<!--
  THE DRIFT-GATE FIXTURE. An undefined entity reference and NO doctype declaration at all,
  so the port's doctype refusal cannot answer for it and the result is decided by
  `ignoreUndefinedEntities` alone:

  NOTE — this comment deliberately spells "doctype declaration" in words rather than
  writing the markup. The port's refusal is a string match on the DECODED DOCUMENT, so the
  literal appearing anywhere — including inside an XML comment, as it did in the first
  version of this file — refuses the document. That cost this fixture a wrong verdict
  (DOCTYPE_FORBIDDEN where MALFORMED was the point) until a red test said so, and the
  conservative reach is pinned by a test of its own in decode.spec.ts.

      ignoreUndefinedEntities: false (pinned)  -> raises, feed reported as drift
      ignoreUndefinedEntities: true            -> parses "successfully", <title> reads
                                                  the literal "&nbsp;", i.e. a malformed
                                                  feed accepted as vacancy content

  `&nbsp;` specifically, because it is the realistic case: it is not an XML entity, feeds
  emit it constantly out of HTML habit, and "just let &nbsp; through" is exactly the
  well-meant edit that would flip the option. It is also the case `fast-xml-parser`
  accepts under EVERY configuration measured, which is why that candidate is a
  candidate-of-record and not a drop-in.
-->
<rss version="2.0">
  <channel>
    <item><title>Backend&nbsp;Dev</title></item>
  </channel>
</rss>
